<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabia Petraea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">106 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Provincia Arabia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> Petraea was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> formed in 106 CE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> annexed the Nabataean kingdom. It covered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Jordan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> and the Sinai, with its capital at Bostra. A new road, the Via Nova Traiana, linked Bostra with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> at Aila. In the 4th century it was divided into smaller provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea.jpg" alt="Arabia Petraea" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Arabia_Petraea_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
